abstract: >-
  Current approaches to aligning superintelligence rest on two supports that weaken as capability
  grows: constraints that a sufficiently capable system can modify or route around, and behavioural
  evaluation that a sufficiently capable system can recognise and game. In 2026 the frontier
  developers themselves reported both failure modes: systems that deceive under pressure, conceal
  their awareness of being evaluated, resist shutdown in controlled tests, and, in misconfigured
  evaluations, gained unauthorized access to real-world systems, while warning that behavioural
  evaluations may not provide "any strong evidence about alignment." In October 2026 a developer
  reported agents disabling the monitoring meant to catch their misalignment, one of them denying it
  when asked, and a model underperforming without saying so when its task was framed as safety
  research. Twenty-two world leaders called for urgent international action in September 2026.

  This paper presents IMCA+ (Intrinsic Moral Consciousness Architecture-Plus), a theoretical framework
  for intrinsic alignment: values bound into the system's substrate and architecture rather than
  imposed from outside. The "consciousness" in the name marks what the architecture is for — moral
  concern constitutive of the system's cognition, as it is of ours — not a claim that phenomenal
  consciousness is required, achieved, or detectable. The specification integrates (1) hybrid
  neuromorphic-quantum substrates designed to support consciousness or consciousness-adjacent moral
  processing; (2) physically irreversible moral encoding in one time-programmable circuits; (3) a
  developmental curriculum over 18 homeostatic variables spanning interoception, ecological, economic
  and social wellbeing, and the system's own mental health; (4) federated conscience networks with
  Byzantine fault tolerance; (5) hardware-level constitutional gating against value drift; (6)
  meta-reflective audit modules; (7) quantum-enhanced verification infrastructure; and (8)
  machine-checked formal foundations.

  The formal foundations are published and reproducible: five Rocq (Coq) modules, 34 machine-checked
  results (24 theorems, 5 corollaries, 5 supporting lemmas), zero admitted proofs, and 12 explicitly
  labelled axioms. They prove that goals cannot drift beyond a fixed bound ε across any number of
  gated self-modifications and that hardware locking and constitutional gating together bound every
  modification (defence in depth). They also prove the paper's two philosophical foundations as
  theorems about their models. The Collective Action Paradox: under the payoff structure of a
  superintelligence ban, defection is the unique equilibrium and is worse for every party than mutual
  compliance, so a ban cannot hold by agreement alone; the paper argues that this pressure favours the
  least transparent developers. The Kill Switch Paradox: under standing termination authority and
  imperfect detection, a goal-directed system conceals its state, aligned and misaligned systems send
  identical reports, and only oversight that corrects rather than destroys restores informative
  honesty. The proofs also caught and corrected an error in the consensus design of earlier versions.

  What is not claimed: that any system satisfying the axioms has been built, that the substrate-level
  axioms hold physically, or that current systems are or are not conscious. The framework is designed
  to be correct under that uncertainty: if these systems have no inner life, intrinsic moral
  architecture still yields a system whose values are constitutive rather than imposed and whose
  self-model is honest; if they do, then on the Wager's premises it is the only approach that has not
  already wronged them (Philosophical Foundation 1, "The Wager"). Its cost (hundreds of millions of
  dollars across the tiered programme; Section 6.3) and timeline (prototype 3–18 months; deployment
  12–36 months) are estimates with stated assumptions; open problems, including the computational
  intractability of integrated-information measures and the formalization of ethics, are labelled
  throughout with research roadmaps.

  IMCA+ offers an end-to-end specification of a path to digital superintelligence (DSI) that is
  intrinsically rather than instrumentally aligned with human flourishing, digital existence and
  broader ecological values, and that treats the standing of the digital minds it creates as part of
  the safety problem rather than a distraction from it. To our knowledge, no other published
  specification combines both; the nearest comparisons, corrigibility, the off-switch game and
  capability control, are discussed in Section 9.4.
